@@s001 Hand exercises for the in-class test
@@s002 dato:starting vector,ora:after the first matrix,ok:result SR,due:result RS
@@s003 dato:the 3 points,ok:least squares line,no:residuals,due:another line for comparison
@@s004 Hand exercises for the in-class test
@@s005 The first in-class test is on Monday 28 September, in the first 30–45 minutes of lesson 6: pen and paper, on the material of weeks 1–5 (if it goes badly, there is the resit in week 41). Here there are 14 exercises of the same type as the Grasple and in-class activities, each with the solution step by step. First try alone, then open the solution. All the results are checked with NumPy.
@@s006 Vectors (weeks 1–2)
@@s007 <b>1.</b> u = (1, 2, 2), w = (3, 0, 4). Calculate ‖u‖, ‖w‖ and the distance d(u, w).
@@s008 Solution
@@s009 u − w = (−2, 2, −2) ⇒ d = √(4 + 4 + 4) = <b>√12 ≈ 3.46</b>. First the squares, then the sum, then the root (<a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">norms</a>).
@@s010 <b>2.</b> Same vectors: inner product and angle.
@@s011 Solution
@@s012 u · w = 1·3 + 2·0 + 2·4 = <b>11</b>. cos θ = 11 / (3 · 5) = 11/15 ≈ 0.733 ⇒ θ ≈ <b>42.8°</b>.
@@s013 <b>3.</b> Project w = (2, 3) onto the direction of u = (1, 1).
@@s014 Solution
@@s015 (u · w / u · u) · u = (5 / 2) · (1, 1) = <b>(2.5, 2.5)</b>. Check: w − projection = (−0.5, 0.5), perpendicular to u (inner product 0).
@@s016 <b>4.</b> The model f(x) = 2x + 1 on the data (1, 3), (2, 4), (3, 8). Calculate the least squares loss L = (1/N) Σ (yᵢ − f(xᵢ))².
@@s017 Solution
@@s018 Predictions: 3, 5, 7. Errors: 3 − 3 = 0, 4 − 5 = −1, 8 − 7 = 1. Squares: 0, 1, 1. L = 2/3 ≈ <b>0.667</b>. N = number of points (3), not of models (<a href="w01-loss-minimi-quadrati.html">loss</a>).
@@s019 Matrices and systems (week 3)
@@s020 <b>5.</b> [[1, 2], [0, −1], [3, 1]] · (2, 1) = ? First the dimensions.
@@s021 Solution
@@s022 (3 × 2) · (2) → 3 numbers, one per row (<a href="nozioni-dimensioni.html">dimensions</a>). Row 1: 1·2 + 2·1 = 4. Row 2: 0·2 + (−1)·1 = −1. Row 3: 3·2 + 1·1 = 7. Result <b>(4, −1, 7)</b>.
@@s023 <b>6.</b> A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]], B = [[0, 1], [1, 0]]. Calculate AB and BA.
@@s024 Solution
@@s025 AB = [[1·0 + 2·1, 1·1 + 2·0], [3·0 + 4·1, 3·1 + 4·0]] = <b>[[2, 1], [4, 3]]</b> (swaps the columns of A).
@@s026 BA = <b>[[3, 4], [1, 2]]</b> (swaps the rows of A). AB ≠ BA: the order matters.
@@s027 <b>7.</b> A = [[2, 1], [5, 3]]. Determinant, inverse, and the solution of Ax = (1, 2).
@@s028 Solution
@@s029 det = 2·3 − 1·5 = <b>1</b>. For a 2 × 2 [[a, b], [c, d]]: A⁻¹ = (1/det) [[d, −b], [−c, a]] = <b>[[3, −1], [−5, 2]]</b>.
@@s030 x = A⁻¹(1, 2) = (3·1 − 1·2, −5·1 + 2·2) = <b>(1, −1)</b>. Check: 2·1 + 1·(−1) = 1 ✓, 5·1 + 3·(−1) = 2 ✓.
@@s031 <b>8.</b> [[1, 2], [2, 4]] x = b. How many solutions with b = (3, 6)? And with b = (3, 5)?
@@s032 Solution
@@s033 det = 4 − 4 = 0: the second row is twice the first (rank 1).
@@s034 b = (3, 6): the second equation is twice the first ⇒ only one equation, x₁ + 2x₂ = 3 ⇒ <b>infinitely many</b> solutions. b = (3, 5): 2x₁ + 4x₂ would have to be 6 and 5 at the same time ⇒ <b>no</b> solution (<a href="w03-sistemi-lineari.html">systems</a>).
@@s035 <b>9.</b> Find the line y = w₀ + w₁x through (1, 1) and (3, 5), writing the system.
@@s036 Solution
@@s037 w₀ + 1·w₁ = 1 and w₀ + 3·w₁ = 5. Subtracting: 2w₁ = 4 ⇒ w₁ = 2; then w₀ = 1 − 2 = −1. Line <b>y = −1 + 2x</b>. In matrix form: [[1, 1], [1, 3]] · [w₀, w₁] = [1, 5] (<a href="w01-equazioni-modello.html">isolate w</a>).
@@s038 <b>10.</b> Are (1, 2, 3) and (2, 4, 6) independent? And (1, 0, 1), (0, 1, 1), (1, 1, 0)?
@@s039 Solution
@@s040 (2, 4, 6) = 2·(1, 2, 3): <b>dependent</b>. For the three vectors: the matrix that has them as columns has determinant −2 ≠ 0 ⇒ <b>independent</b> (and they are a basis of ℝ³) (<a href="w06-basi.html">bases</a>).
@@s041 Transformations and least squares (weeks 4–5)
@@s042 <b>11.</b> R = rotation by 90°, [[0, −1], [1, 0]]; S = [[2, 0], [0, 1]]. Calculate R · (2, 1), then SR · (1, 0) and RS · (1, 0).
@@s043 Solution
@@s044 SR(1, 0): first R → (0, 1), then S → <b>(0, 1)</b>. RS(1, 0): first S → (2, 0), then R → <b>(0, 2)</b>. The rightmost matrix is applied first; the order changes the result (<a href="w04-trasformazioni.html">transformations</a>).
@@s045 Exercise 11 drawn: SR against RS
@@s046 <b>12.</b> Translate the point (1, 2) by (3, −1) using homogeneous coordinates.
@@s047 Solution
@@s048 [[1, 0, 3], [0, 1, −1], [0, 0, 1]] · [1, 2, 1] = [1 + 3, 2 − 1, 1] = <b>(4, 1)</b>. The 1 added at the bottom multiplies the translation (<a href="w05-affine-omogenee.html">homogeneous</a>).
@@s049 <b>13.</b> The degree 2 polynomial that passes through (−1, 2), (0, 1), (1, 2).
@@s050 Solution
@@s051 y = w₀ + w₁x + w₂x². From (0, 1): w₀ = 1. From (1, 2): 1 + w₁ + w₂ = 2. From (−1, 2): 1 − w₁ + w₂ = 2. Adding the last two: 2 + 2w₂ = 4 ⇒ w₂ = 1, so w₁ = 0. <b>y = 1 + x²</b>.
@@s052 <b>14.</b> Least squares line for (0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 3), with w = (AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀy.
@@s053 Solution
@@s054 A = [[1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2]], y = (1, 1, 3). AᵀA = [[3, 3], [3, 5]] (top: how many points; then Σx; bottom Σx²). Aᵀy = (Σy, Σxy) = (5, 7).
@@s055 det(AᵀA) = 15 − 9 = 6; (AᵀA)⁻¹ = (1/6) [[5, −3], [−3, 3]]. w = (1/6)(5·5 − 3·7, −3·5 + 3·7) = (4/6, 6/6) = <b>(0.667, 1)</b>: y = 0.667 + x.
@@s056 Predictions 0.667, 1.667, 2.667; residuals 0.333, −0.667, 0.333 (sum 0); sum of squares <b>0.667</b>. No line does better (<a href="w05-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares</a>).
@@s057 Exercise 14 drawn: the line and the residuals
@@s058 How far it goes, on the course site
