@@s082 Because also three exercises of W06
@@s083 The exercises of week N concern the lesson of the week before. The <b>basis</b> is a topic of lesson 5 (<i>Kernels and Basis</i>) and <b>linear independence</b> was already in the in-class of week 4: those three Grasple fall within lessons 1&ndash;5.
@@s084 Review order
@@s085 (1) The <b>in-class 1&ndash;4</b> (<code>www/05-inclass/</code>): hand exercises done in class, the format closest to the test. 1 = distances, loss, model equations &middot; 2 = linear combinations, matrix &times; vector, matrix &times; matrix &middot; 3 = design matrix and weights with the inverse &middot; 4 = linear independence, bases and projections. (2) The Grasple of the <i>Inside</i> column. (3) The Refresh Questions of W02&ndash;W05, orally. (4) The exercises above.
@@s086 It is an estimate
@@s087 The table is derived from the structure of the course, not from an official list. For confirmation: the guide says you can write to Dan <b>by the day before the lesson</b> to ask which topics to review in the initial session, which is exactly that of the test.
@@s088 Up to which exercises of the Week Material does the test go?
@@s089 Up to W05 included, plus the first three Grasple of W06 (vector spaces, linear independence, bases and projections). They are inside the Grasple and the hand exercises; of the Python notebooks the concept is needed, not the code.
@@s090 Before the test
@@s091 Three checks that save points
@@s092 (1) <b>Dimensions</b> before every product. (2) <b>Put the solution back into the equations</b> (as in exercises 7 and 9): if it does not work, the error is in the calculations. (3) <b>Order of operations</b>: in the losses first the square of each error, then the sum, then the division by N.
